s and is it really as expensive  as people think I don't think I really I  the economic model is not is on there  but it's really not it's really not that  expensive I mean to me it's it's the  benefits far outweigh the costs and you  know we subsidize a portion but it's not  you know it's it's not in any way  prohibitive or to our business and  they've been doing it you know  Patagonia's been doing it since they  were a small company so it's just built  into the business model yeah an  incredible inspiration and I hope that  one that we'll all take with us as  future leaders and implement so if we  zoom out from Patagonia's business and  culture and talk a bit more about your  views on corporate leadership  particularly in the 21st century with  all of the problems that we're facing  under your leadership Patagonia has  become much more political you mentioned  earlier you even sued the Trump  administration for its plans to roll  back public lands  why did you ramp up your political  activism and in the lead up to 2020 is  this something that all business leaders  should be thinking about well I think  we've seen a lot I mean when when the  administration pulled out of the Paris  climate Accord we're the only major  country not in the Paris climate Accord  it's a it's a disgrace and you know you  saw business leaders step up and you  know sign it sign a document saying  we're still in we're in the Accord we're  gonna keep working towards those goals  and I think that was the first time I  was like wow business is waking up and  they're starting to really you know  participate and take a stand and you  know are a reaction to what this  administration has done is I think  proportional given that given that the  company's always been about protecting  wild places and protecting the  environment  you know when Trump signs away three  million acres of public land with a  stroke of a pen we're not going to sit  silent about that and it was you know it  was a definitely 