ns from  your career and trajectory and many of  us in this room at Stanford hope to have  found companies we're known for  attracting aspiring entrepreneurs but  there's this other equally powerful path  of working alongside founders and  helping to scale the vision and impact  which is very much what you've done  alongside Yvonne she nod what's the most  important thing that you've learned from  working with him oh man there's so many  things  well he's completely fearless whenever  he makes a decision he doesn't care what  anyone says or thinks or he's just  completely fearless he keeps going you  know and you might get some noise over  here complaints here there but you know  just he doesn't let it bother him he  just he keeps he keeps moving forward  and and he's he's very self examining  it's really part of the company culture  to live an examined life and all of us I  feel like do that and we're all curious  people and yeah and I think that in  quality he's he always has really really  focused on quality and I think he's  right to always bring it back to that  because at the end of the day we're  selling a product and we want that  product to be the highest possible  quality and we want the relationship  with the customer I'm lucky enough to  have some of our Palo Alto store  employees here today and it's like they  do the really hard work of like  expressing the brand through themselves  as people and that is always been really  important you know aspect of the brand  this idea of living a self examined life  I wanted to ask you about this you have  a strong set of inner values that guide  you and anchored in sort of your  Buddhist and meditation practice that  you mentioned earlier  what role does this play in your  leadership and what advice would you  share with us for cultivating that  sense of self well I think it's really  good always to be self examining and to  be curious about differences and you  know we become so polarized as a country  in so many