eople or difficult business conditions  or whatever it may be and and you learn  a lot about yourself if you're a curious  person and you know like I've learned I  learned a lot from like the bad bosses  that I've had I learned more probably  from the bad bosses than I have from the  good bosses you know there are things  like that that in retrospect when you  look back on it you realize oh yeah I  like didn't ever want to be like that or  I did you know I want to incorporate  this or that or yeah I mean I think yeah  I think that's that's sort of the way I  see it yeah absolutely well we wanted to  leave time today for questions from the  audience so I'm going to turn it over  now to some audience members who have  questions for you great I am Maya grant  and I'm an MBA too and then also doing  the Masters of Science in environmental  studies so if we want to save our home  planet we need to get a heck of a lot of  people involved but the environmental  movement is often associated with a  certain demographic often wealthier  often white and frequently women so what  is Patagonia doing and what can we do as  Future Business Leaders to push that  movement to a greater representation and  kind of include more people in the  narrative yeah  I think that's a really good point it's  a really good good question so what we  started doing this a few years ago with  with our our grassroots giving because  that seemed like the fastest way that we  could activate it and we started just  looking for groups that were outside of  our normals sort of outdoor cohort you  know and and a lot of these  relationships formed over time with just  because Yvonne met people along the way  and they were protecting certain places  and so that's sort of how the movement  grew and and so we took it more into  protecting area  that maybe aren't the grand outdoor  places but it's a cool group of you know  latina moms in LA that are doing you  know that are doing air quality testing  like we wouldn't have even known about  that group before you know and so we  started just bringing in these we  started having kind of conferences with  all these different NGOs and and just  just like making more connections  because we're all really connected in  the same movement and it's just about  like making the connections stronger and  deeper and I think that's the way that  we're looking at it and we're also you  know exchanging sharing learning a lot  from from that process as well so hi  guys  thank you for being here my name is  Ellery and I'm also a second year I'm  organizing the climate conference with  Maya so I'm very excited about this  topic and given Patagonia's 2025  commitment to being carbon neutral and  the complicated history of carbon  offsets  how does Patagonia think about choosing  office offsets that truly deliver when  they are honest and then secondarily how  can we collectively hold Patagonia and  other companies like Microsoft and  JetBlue to the commitments they make  yeah this is a really good question  because like when I first looked it up I  did I didn't really know much about  offsets but when we first started  looking at offsets as a part of our  carbon plan someone told me I could  spend a hundred thousand dollars and I  could offset all of our you know all of  our carbon in the US and I was like that  makes absolutely no sense to me like  just in a practical level that makes  zero sense I don't know where that  hundred thousand dollars is going but it  doesn't seem to be going for what it's  you know really going for so we're  really looking for like absolute  reductions you know by implementing like  implementing solar grids or working on  energy deals or working on converting  factories that are using coal to using  renewable energy and that's the way  we're we're looking at it offsets  there's some offsets that are that are  good they're they're they're  conservation mostly conservation related  the stuff that we're looking at and I  find that to be a little bit you know to  make more sense because conserved land  a carbon sink and it really you know if  it's not conserved it's likely to be  disrupted and destroyed but the market  itself is you know you I think you have  to be very careful about how people get  to that goal and are they getting to it  in a real way so just just keep asking  those deep questions because you know  that's how you hold people accountable  really you know I rose thanks for being  here  I'm Carolyn Lane a first year MBA  student and I have a question about kind  of the global ambition of Patagonia  because we all know Pentagon is in the  business of saving home planet and it's  such a global crisis and imperative  especially for the developing countries  where you you're we're gonna see you  know another bill in middle class  entering the world stage when 2025 so  what is the vision and ambition for  Pentagon yet on the global stage do you  see more aggressive expansion of their  global footprint and how do you balance  that with your anti growth strategy yeah  so it's not really about growing our  footprint so much is like activating our  community in outside of the US region  and we've been in you know we've been in  Japan for almost 30 years we've had a  business in Europe from us 30 years and  in South America and Australia and and  in all those places what we're trying to  do is first of all give to grassroots  organizations that are really doing  things on the ground and that creates  kind of a community for us to work with  and it's one of the biggest changes that  I feel like in my tenure CEO I feel  really proud of is making that mission  much more global and and we use a tool  that we introduced a few years ago  called Patagonia action works which are  now launching around the globe and it  really is to connect our customers with  our causes because a lot of our  customers just want to know what can I  do like you know can I donate time  should I donate